# A value-headed call whose arity is wrong AND whose argument fails. The
# arity check answers first on every engine: a wrong-arity call is a broken
# program, and letting the failing argument answer instead hides the break
# exactly when a value happens to fail.
#
# The checker cannot catch it. A function-typed parameter's arity is not read
# at the call site, so `two_b lam1 3 "second"` compiles and the dispatch
# happens at run time — which is what makes this a differential question
# rather than a diagnostic one.
#
# Recorded as OPEN and believed unreachable on 2026-09-02: "no program in the
# corpus reaches it and I did not find a way to write one". This is one.
# `k_call{n}` tested every argument for failure before it tested arity, so
# native answered `second` and exited 0 where the oracle died here. Both
# orders are the same now, and this fixture is what says so.
fn boom what
  err what

fn two_b g a what
  g a (boom what)

lam1 = (x -> x * 10)

bad = two_b lam1 3 "second"

pub play = print "{bad}"
